## When to parallelize?

*(from above github link)*

It's not as simple as it may seem. While in theory each added processor would linearly increase the throughput of a computation, there is overhead that reduces that efficiency.

For example, the code and, importantly, the data need to be copied to each additional CPU, and this takes time and bandwidth. Plus, new processes and/or threads need to be created by the operating system, which also takes time.

This overhead reduces the efficiency enough that realistic performance gains are much less than theoretical, and usually do not scale linearly as a function of processing power. For example, if the time that a computation takes is short, then the overhead of setting up these additional resources may actually overwhelm any advantages of the additional processing power, and the computation could potentially take longer!

so.........

When you have a list of repetitive tasks, you may be able to speed it up by adding more computing power. If each task is completely independent of the others, then it is a prime candidate for executing those tasks in parallel, each on its own core.

## Parallel loops!

### A basic loop

do really basic stuff..

```{r, warning = FALSE}

for (i in 1:10) {
  
  a <- i * i
  
}

```

### but lets time it

```{r, warning = FALSE}

start.time <- Sys.time()

x <- vector()

for (i in 1:10000){
  
  x[i] <- i * i
  
}

end.time <- Sys.time()

(time.taken <- end.time - start.time)

```

### Now lets use most our cores

Firstly we need to tell R how many cores we want it to use. It probably is not wise to use all the cores as windows and other processes need some space too!

For me, depending on what other work I am doing I tend to use 70% to 90% of my cores (15 or 19)

To do this we:

```{r, warning = FALSE}

(total_cores <- detectCores())

cl <- parallel::makeCluster(total_cores - 2)
doParallel::registerDoParallel(cl)

```

Okay now we have told R was can run the loop again, except using the correct syntax for parallel, its simple!

And include the time cost of registering the cores..

cl \<- parallel::makeCluster(18)

doParallel::registerDoParallel(cl)

